# nr-relay-webpush-http: what the wiring applies (nothing here was compiled: cargo was not run)

No hot file is edited: no Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock or workspace-hack edit, no protocol row, and SERVICE_PENDING in compose_flip.rs
loses no name (Web Push is relay-side; there is no protocol method). mixdog-relay/Cargo.toml already carries every crate used;
tests/web_push_http.rs is auto-discovered like tests/web_push.rs (it uses no gated helper).

## What the task that assembles the relay (nr-relay-server) does

1. Build the identity once: `let web_push = WebPush::open(&data_dir, Arc::new(ReqwestClient::new()?)).map(Arc::new)` (None when no identity
   could be loaded or minted: then Web Push is off, the routes answer 404, no capability is offered).
2. After `Hub::new(...)`: `if let Some(web_push) = &web_push { mixdog_relay::web_push::frames::register(&hub, web_push.clone())?; }`.
   This registers, on `hub.dispatch()`: the `webPush` capability, the `webpush-subscriptions` connect frame, the handlers of
   `webpush-ready` and `webpush-send` (answered with `webpush-result`, the leg is not held while the push service answers) and one
   RevokeHook (revoke-device drops the PC's associations and readiness; revoke-client drops that browser's and publishes the PC's new set).
   Register it once, beside the native-push registrations: a second registration of the two frame types returns `Rejected::Taken`.
3. In the request router, before static files: `if web_push::http::is_web_push_request(&request.url) { webpush_endpoint.handle(request, response).await }`
   with `WebPushEndpoint::new(web_push.clone(), hub.clone(), unauthorized_limiter.clone())`; `unauthorized_limiter` is the relay's one
   `Arc<Mutex<RateLimiter>>` shared with its other routes (the same one `reject_unauthorized_text` takes by &mut).
4. `frames::publish(&hub, &web_push, device_id)` is `webPush.publish(deviceId)` for any other caller that changes a registry.

